Therapist: Hi both, thanks for making time this week. How are you both doing coming in today?
Client: Honestly, we had a pretty rough week. I'm Priya, by the way, in case the connection dropped your video again like last time.
Client: It's Mike here too. Yeah, it's been rough. We had a big fight Sunday night about the Emma and Noah's school pickup schedule.
Therapist: Let's start there, since it sounds like it's still pretty fresh. Priya, can you walk me through what happened from your side?
Client: Sure. Mike was supposed to get the kids from school because I had a late meeting, and he forgot, so the school called me instead. I had to step out of an important call to go get them.
Client: I didn't forget, I told her that morning my meeting ran long and I texted her.
Client: You texted me forty minutes after pickup time, Mike, which doesn't help me when I'm already in the car.
Therapist: I can hear this is still a live wound for both of you. Mike, what was going on for you that morning that led to the late text?
Client: Work has just been insane lately. I've been distracted, and I know that's not an excuse, but it's the truth.
Client: It's been insane for both of us. I'm not the only one with a job.
Therapist: It sounds like there's a shared feeling underneath this of both of you feeling like your own pressures aren't being seen by the other person.
Client: That's exactly it, actually. I feel like my job never gets taken as seriously as his.
Client: That's not true, I do take it seriously.
Client: Sorry — sorry, I'm getting a little choked up. This isn't just about Sunday, it's about feeling like the default parent every time something slips.
Therapist: Take your time. That sounds like it's touching something bigger than just one missed pickup.
Client: Yeah. I love our life together, I just feel like I'm the backup plan for everything, all the time.
Therapist: Mike, hearing her say that, what comes up for you?
Client: Honestly, I didn't realize it felt that heavy for her. I thought we were more balanced than that.
Therapist: I want to try something with both of you right now, called the speaker-listener technique. One of you speaks, using "I" statements, and the other's only job is to reflect back what they heard, before responding with their own view. Priya, would you like to go first?
Client: Sure. I feel like I carry the mental load of the kids' schedules by default, and when something slips, it becomes my emergency to fix, even when it wasn't my mistake.
Therapist: Mike, can you reflect back what you heard her say, before responding?
Client: I heard that she feels like she's the default person for the kids' schedule, and that when something goes wrong, it becomes her problem to solve even if it wasn't her fault.
Therapist: That's a strong, accurate reflection. Priya, did that land the way you meant it?
Client: Yeah, actually, that felt like he really heard it, which doesn't always happen when we're just arguing about it directly.
Therapist: Mike, now it's your turn to share, using an "I" statement, and Priya, your job is just to reflect.
Client: I feel like I'm trying, and when the mistakes get pointed out without any of the effort being acknowledged, it makes me want to shut down instead of engage.
Client: I heard that you feel like you're trying, and that when you only hear about the mistakes and not the effort, it makes you want to shut down instead of stay engaged.
Therapist: Mike, how did it feel to have that reflected back accurately?
Client: Better than I expected, honestly. It's different hearing it said back instead of just defending myself in the moment.
Therapist: That's the value of slowing the exchange down like this — it interrupts the usual defend-and-counter pattern that probably happens in the moment on a Sunday night. I'd like you both to practice this technique once this week, even on something small, just to build the muscle before you need it for something bigger.
Client: We could try it on the grocery list argument we have every single week.
Client: That's actually a good low-stakes one to start with.
Therapist: Perfect, that's exactly the kind of low-stakes practice that makes the technique available later for the harder conversations. I also want to name something for the record — it sounds like this pickup schedule issue is a recurring source of tension, not a one-time thing. Would you both be open to actually redesigning the schedule together, maybe using a shared calendar, rather than relying on morning texts?
Client: We've talked about doing that before and never actually set it up.
Client: I can set that up this week, honestly, now that we're actually talking about it instead of just fighting about it.
Therapist: That would be a great concrete follow-up to bring back next time. One last thing — your anniversary is coming up in August, if I remember right. How are you both feeling about that, given everything else going on?
Client: We usually do a small trip, just the two of us, while my mom watches Emma and Noah.
Client: I actually want to keep that plan this year. I think we need it more than usual.
Therapist: That sounds like a meaningful thing to protect, especially given how much tension has built up around the day-to-day logistics lately. To summarize today — we used the speaker-listener technique on the pickup schedule conflict, you're going to practice it once this week on something lower-stakes, Mike's setting up a shared calendar for pickups, and you're keeping the anniversary trip plan in August.
Client: That feels like real progress, actually. More than I expected walking in today.
Client: Same here. Thanks for slowing us down, we clearly needed it.
Therapist: I'm glad today felt useful. I'll see you both same time next week.
Client: Sounds good, thank you.
Client: Thanks, see you then.
